**Description**: This study quantifies the transition from Gaussian to anomalous diffusion of vortex centroids within a 128³ periodic domain by implementing a Galilean-invariant tracking framework that subtracts the large-scale advective flow field (low-pass filtered at $n \leq 3$) from the velocity gradient tensor. Vortices are isolated using a dynamic Q-criterion threshold, defined as $Q > \text{mean}(Q) + 2\sigma_Q$, to mitigate background turbulence noise and ensure topological stability. Only trajectories persisting for $\geq 10$ snapshots are retained, with displacements calculated using the minimum-image convention to account for periodic boundaries. We compute the mean squared displacement (MSD) and the probability density functions of step sizes $\Delta r$ for these filtered trajectories to distinguish between Brownian motion and Lévy flights. Crucially, the diffusion exponent $\alpha$ is mapped against the local instantaneous energy dissipation rate $\epsilon \approx 2\nu S_{ij}S_{ij}$ to determine if superdiffusive transport is driven by proximity to intense dissipative structures. The final analysis will synthesize 3D trajectory visualizations, log-log MSD plots, and heavy-tailed distribution fits to provide a robust statistical classification of vortex dynamics in subsonic driven turbulence.


