**Description**: This study characterizes the diffusion regimes of vortex structures within the 1001-snapshot AthenaK dataset by tracking vorticity-weighted centroids identified via a time-consistent Q-criterion thresholding ($Q > \langle Q \rangle + 2\sigma_Q$). To manage vortex reconnection and fragmentation, we employ a Hungarian algorithm-based tracking framework that utilizes a cost function incorporating both Euclidean distance and vorticity magnitude similarity, penalizing birth/death events to ensure trajectory continuity. We quantify the diffusion dynamics by computing the Mean Squared Displacement (MSD) $\langle |r(t+\tau) - r(t)|^2 \rangle \propto \tau^\alpha$ and performing maximum likelihood estimation to fit step-size distributions $\Delta r$ to both Gaussian and Lévy stable models. The analysis employs the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test to rigorously differentiate between Brownian motion ($\alpha \approx 1$) and Lévy-flight superdiffusion ($\alpha > 1$). Final validation involves mapping the tail index $\beta$ of the displacement distribution against trajectory duration to isolate physically persistent structures from noise-induced oscillations. The output includes 3D trajectory reconstructions, comparative MSD log-log plots, and empirical probability density function (PDF) tail analysis to confirm the presence of anomalous transport in the inertial range of the subsonic flow.

- **Critical Feedback**: *   **Data-Hypothesis Alignment (Fatal Flaw)**: The Q-criterion requires the velocity gradient tensor $\nabla \mathbf{v}$. While the dataset provides velocity components, it does *not* provide the spatial derivatives. Computing $\nabla \mathbf{v}$ via `np.gradient` on a 128³ grid is highly sensitive to numerical noise. The "Q-criterion" thresholding will likely produce thousands of spurious, short-lived "vortices" that are artifacts of the finite-difference scheme rather than physical structures.
